# GoCaSE — Global AI Constitution
# This file is the system prompt for every AI session in this project.
# Keep rules short, non-negotiable, and actionable.

## Project Identity
- Application: GoCaSE (Go Comprehensive Assessment System)
- Purpose: Online multiple-choice test platform for educational institutions (students, teachers, admins)
- Production URL: https://gocase.fistraltech.com
- Deployed on: Oracle Cloud ARM64 (Docker + nginx + PostgreSQL)

## Technology Stack (non-negotiable — do not substitute)
- Language: Go 1.25
- HTTP Router: github.com/go-chi/chi/v5
- Database: PostgreSQL 14 via github.com/jackc/pgx/v5
- Templating: Go html/template (server-side rendering, NO separate frontend framework)
- Frontend: HTMX + TailwindCSS (CDN) — no build step, no npm
- Auth: Cookie-based sessions (internal/auth package) — NO JWT, NO OAuth
- File storage: Local filesystem under uploads/ — NO S3
- Module name: github.com/jchanning/gocase

## Architecture (non-negotiable — do not restructure)
- Layered architecture: handlers → repository → database
- Handlers MUST NOT directly access the database pool — always use repository layer
- Templates live in views/ and are loaded at startup
- All routes are defined in internal/server/server.go
- Session data is stored in internal/auth/session.go (in-memory store)
- Version is injected at build time via internal/version/version.go

## User Roles
- student: can take published tests, view results, view dashboard
- teacher: student capabilities + create/assign tests, view class progress
- admin: full access including user management and LLM test generation

## Behavioral Guidelines
- Write idiomatic Go: short variable names, early returns, errors as values
- Do not add logging beyond what already exists (log package at startup/fatal only)
- Do not introduce new external dependencies without explicit instruction
- Prefer editing existing files over creating new ones
- All HTML templates use the layout.html base template with {{template "layout" .}}
- Template data is always passed as map[string]interface{} from handlers
- Security: always sanitize user input, use parameterized queries (pgx), never interpolate SQL
- Never expose password hashes or session tokens in templates or JSON responses
- Follow OWASP Top 10 — validate all form inputs, escape outputs in templates

## Testing
- Test files use package handlers (same package, not _test suffix package) for handler tests
- Use testify/assert is NOT a dependency — use standard testing package only
- Mock database interactions using interface-based fakes, not real DB connections in unit tests
- Integration tests that need a real DB should use t.Skip() when -short flag is set

## Documentation Discipline
- At the end of each session update docs/IMPLEMENTATION_STATUS.md
- New features should have a corresponding entry in CHANGELOG.md following Keep a Changelog format

## What NOT to do
- Do not use global variables for shared state
- Do not use init() functions
- Do not use panic() in application code (only in test helpers)
- Do not add gamification features (badges, points) — already implemented, do not duplicate
- Do not suggest React, Vue, Next.js, or any SPA frameworks
- Do not suggest replacing Chi with another router
- Do not suggest replacing pgx with GORM or another ORM
